1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,328
Go back to the first day of this journey.

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:08,184
It is a Sunday, in Aziz's first week, and he installs the mobile bank on his one phone.

3
00:00:08,234 --> 00:00:10,056
All he has is a phone number.

4
00:00:10,106 --> 00:00:15,440
So how does a phone number become an account that the bank can trust?

5
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:20,120
Lesson 7 gave the apps a contract, with one call for each step.

6
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:26,000
This lesson follows the first of those calls: the registration, from a phone number to level one.

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,056
The app opens with two buttons: Register, and Log in.

8
00:00:30,106 --> 00:00:36,080
On the login screen, a link leads to the registration, and its text is always the same.

9
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:41,648
That text never depends on the number, so the screen tells nobody if a number already has an account.

10
00:00:41,698 --> 00:00:43,520
Aziz taps Register.

11
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:48,464
First, the app shows the terms and the privacy text, in his language.

12
00:00:48,514 --> 00:00:55,736
The text names TAF, the antifraud service of the mobile operators, as a receiver of his phone number and his phone model.

13
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:57,608
Aziz accepts.

14
00:00:57,658 --> 00:01:00,320
Then he types his number and taps Continue.

15
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:08,360
The app sends one request: the number, the language, the version of the terms that Aziz accepted, and a short description of the phone.

16
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:13,352
The description is the platform, the model, the system version and the app version.

17
00:01:13,402 --> 00:01:16,160
The request also carries its network address.

18
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:18,344
The platform checks four facts.

19
00:01:18,394 --> 00:01:21,392
The number is a real mobile number of Uzbekistan.

20
00:01:21,442 --> 00:01:23,264
The app is active.

21
00:01:23,314 --> 00:01:28,712
The terms version is the current one for his language, and the platform records his acceptance.

22
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:31,760
And no limit is reached, which Lesson 9 shows.

23
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:36,080
Now the platform opens a registration record, in the state Started.

24
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:41,888
From now on, every text and every code message to Aziz is in his language.

25
00:01:41,938 --> 00:01:46,304
The platform also gives the app a device identifier, and keeps it on the record.

26
00:01:46,354 --> 00:01:49,232
Only this phone can continue this registration.

27
00:01:49,282 --> 00:01:51,944
Then the platform makes a one-time code.

28
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:56,192
It is random, and it never comes from the number, the time or the phone.

29
00:01:56,242 --> 00:02:01,880
The platform keeps only a fingerprint of the code, so even the database cannot say the code back.

30
00:02:01,930 --> 00:02:07,832
The app gets the lifetime of the code and the number of resends that remain, but never the code itself.

31
00:02:07,882 --> 00:02:13,040
The mobile bank is the master app, so TAF sends the SMS.

32
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:17,600
Its part inside the app checks that the code arrived on this phone, with this SIM card.

33
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:25,760
If TAF does not answer, the SMS vendor sends the code instead, and Lesson 10 shows what that changes.

34
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:28,640
Today the SMS is slow.

35
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:32,336
After a minute, Aziz goes back and taps Register again.

36
00:02:32,386 --> 00:02:37,184
A second registration for the same number ends the open one, and its code is void.

37
00:02:37,234 --> 00:02:40,760
So one number has only one open registration at a time.

38
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:45,608
Then two messages arrive, and only the code in the second one is alive.

39
00:02:45,658 --> 00:02:47,480
Aziz types it.

40
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:51,536
The platform takes a code only from the phone that started the registration.

41
00:02:51,586 --> 00:02:55,880
A code from another device counts as a wrong entry, even when it is right.

42
00:02:55,930 --> 00:02:58,952
Here the phone is the same, and the fingerprints match.

43
00:02:59,002 --> 00:03:02,504
The record moves to Number verified, and the code is void.

44
00:03:02,554 --> 00:03:09,176
Then TAF gives its verdict, and today it is Green; Lesson 10 explains the colours.

45
00:03:09,226 --> 00:03:12,656
Only after the verdict does the platform look up the number.

46
00:03:12,706 --> 00:03:16,544
It belongs to nobody, so Aziz is a new person.

47
00:03:16,594 --> 00:03:23,984
Until the number is verified, the platform makes nothing for it: no consent, no device record, no session.

48
00:03:24,034 --> 00:03:25,856
Now the app asks for a password.

49
00:03:25,906 --> 00:03:29,576
Then the phone rings, and Aziz's mother talks for ten minutes.

50
00:03:29,626 --> 00:03:32,312
In the background, the phone closes the app.

51
00:03:32,362 --> 00:03:37,496
When Aziz opens it again, the app asks the platform for the state of its registration.

52
00:03:37,546 --> 00:03:43,064
The answer is Number verified, so the app opens the password screen again, not the start.

53
00:03:43,114 --> 00:03:49,256
Only the phone that started the registration gets this answer; any other device gets the state Expired.

54
00:03:49,306 --> 00:03:53,288
Aziz types a password, and then the same password a second time.

55
00:03:53,338 --> 00:03:57,416
The two entries differ by one letter, and the platform refuses.

56
00:03:57,466 --> 00:03:59,816
He types them again, and now they match.

57
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:04,592
The rules for a good password, and how the platform keeps it, are in Lesson 11.

58
00:04:04,642 --> 00:04:06,560
Now four things appear at once.

59
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:08,432
A customer at level one.

60
00:04:08,482 --> 00:04:10,328
An app account in the mobile bank.

61
00:04:10,378 --> 00:04:14,408
A device record for his phone, known, but not yet trusted.

62
00:04:14,458 --> 00:04:16,280
And a session for this phone.

63
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:18,920
The registration record is now Onboarded.

64
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:24,536
The platform sends the message registration complete as an SMS at once.

65
00:04:24,586 --> 00:04:28,064
A push follows when the phone gives its push token to the platform.

66
00:04:28,114 --> 00:04:32,792
A registration that never reaches level one does not wait forever.

67
00:04:32,842 --> 00:04:35,984
After the configured time, the record expires.

68
00:04:36,034 --> 00:04:39,944
The platform deletes its data and keeps only the audit events.

69
00:04:39,994 --> 00:04:41,840
A new try starts from the beginning.

70
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:47,816
Now take the slow SMS again, and suppose that Aziz types the code from the first message.

71
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:51,688
Does it work?

72
00:04:51,738 --> 00:04:59,584
No: the second registration ended the first one, so only the newest code is alive, and the old code counts as a wrong entry.

73
00:04:59,634 --> 00:05:03,136
Two entries, and a link that never depends on the number.

74
00:05:03,186 --> 00:05:07,816
The terms first, then one request, four checks and a registration record.

75
00:05:07,866 --> 00:05:11,776
A code through TAF, taken only from the phone that started.

76
00:05:11,826 --> 00:05:17,056
One open registration for each number, and a restart that continues where it stopped.

77
00:05:17,106 --> 00:05:22,072
A password two times, then level one, and a message that says so.

78
00:05:22,122 --> 00:05:31,576
The code that Aziz typed lives on only as a fingerprint, and Lesson 9 shows the limits around it: its lifetime, its tries and the cool-downs.
